  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Clearing today as warming trend begins, then late \nweek fronts bring rain, windy conditions, and cooler temperatures.\n\nThe remnant cutoff low of what was once Hurricane Isaias is slowly \nspinning over the Mid-Atlantic early this morning. This low is going \nto push offshore through the afternoon. Lingering showers this \nmorning, some of which are producing moderate to heavy rain, come to \nan end from west to east as northwest winds begin to increase. While \nclouds won't clear till this afternoon, there is going to be more \nsunshine peaking through. This allows temps to reach the low to mid \n70s. Wind gusts around 20-25mph this morning to early afternoon.\n\nMid-level ridging builds over the region through the middle of the \nweek. Dry and sunnier conditions are expected, though that also \nbrings the return of well above temperatures. Highs reach around \n80F on Tuesday and mid 80s on Wednesday. \n\nA low pressure system tracks from the Plains towards the Great Lakes \nWednesday night into Thursday. Its associated warm front lifts north \ninto the forecast area, possibly producing a few showers Wednesday \nnight. As the low pressure system tracks northeast into Canada by \nthe end of the week, the associated cold front pushes across the \nforecast area in the wake of the aforementioned warm front. This \nfrontal system brings increased precipitation chances Thursday and \nFriday. Additionally, high temperatures in the 60s and 70s Thursday \nand Friday cool to the 40s and 50s on Saturday in the wake of the \ncold front. \n\nBehind the frontal system, surface high pressure builds over the \ncentral CONUS beginning on Friday before shifting eastward. A tight \npressure gradient between the surface high and the departing low \npressure system leads to breezy conditions across the forecast area \nFriday night into Saturday. Northwest winds gust 20 to 30 knots, \nwith higher elevations gusting up to 40 knots. Wind headlines may be \nneeded across the higher elevations but we will continue to monitor. \nIt's also worth mentioning that with gusty northwest winds, upslope \nsnow showers are possible along the Alleghenies on Saturday with \nhigh temperatures staying in the 30s across the ridges. Little to no \naccumulation is expected although the first flakes of the season are \npossible.",
